2603.03637v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 4 Mar 2026]\nL9: # Title:Image-based Prompt Injection: Hijacking Multimodal LLMs through Visually Embedded Adversarial Instructions\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Neha Nagaraja , cite6†Lan Zhang , cite7†Zhilong Wang , cite8†Bo Zhang , cite9†Pawan Patil L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Image-based Prompt Injection: Hijacking Multimodal LLMs through Visually Embedded Adversarial Instructions, by Neha Nagaraja and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) integrate vision and text to power applications, but this integration introduces new vulnerabilities. We study Image-based Prompt Injection (IPI), a black-box attack in which adversarial instructions are embedded into natural images to override model behavior.\nL17: Our end-to-end IPI pipeline incorporates segmentation-based region selection, adaptive font scaling, and background-aware rendering to conceal prompts from human perception while preserving model interpretability. Using the COCO dataset and GPT-4-turbo, we evaluate 12 adversarial prompt strategies and multiple embedding configurations.\nL18: The results show that IPI can reliably manipulate the output of the model, with the most effective configuration achieving up to 64\\% attack success under stealth constraints. These findings highlight IPI as a practical threat in black-box settings and underscore the need for defenses against multimodal prompt injection.\nL19: Comments:  | 7 pages, published in 2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM), Vienna, Austria\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2603.03637 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2603.03637v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.03637†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | 2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM), Vienna, Austria, 2025, pp. 916-922\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite15†https://doi.org/10.1109/FLLM67465.2025.11391218†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Neha Nagaraja [cite16†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Wed, 4 Mar 2026 01:58:08 UTC (587 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Image-based Prompt Injection: Hijacking Multimodal LLMs through Visually Embedded Adversarial Instructions, by Neha Nagaraja and 4 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite10†View PDF L38:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite17†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite18†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CV\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-03 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: cite25†cs.AI L54: cite26†cs.CR L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \nL68: loading...\nL69: \nL70: Data provided by:\nL71: \nL72: ### Bookmark\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL75: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Bib